  ;; `join`, alone, because of what it costs the modules it would otherwise
  ;; share a file with.
  :kotoba.string.join
  {:path "lang/compat/kotoba/string/join.kotoba"
   :provides #{join}
   :replaces {join clojure.string/join}
   :equivalence :exact
   :why-its-own-namespace "join reads a [:list :string] with typed-list-nth, and a module containing that operation is refused WHOLE by an amu that does not lower it -- a caller that mentions only starts-with? cannot build. Keeping it here means only its own callers need the newer amu, instead of the 74 modules that require clojure.string."
   :measured
   {:as-of "2026-09-08"
    :blast-radius "measured both directions with join still in clojure.string. Probe: (ns probe-str (:require [clojure.string :as s]) (:export [main])) (defn main [] :i64 (if (s/starts-with? \"abc\" \"ab\") 1 0)). At amu 9092ee34, --target wasm32 is exit 70 :error :wasm-typed-lowering \"unsupported typed Wasm expression\"; the same tree with join and join-from deleted and the name dropped from :export is exit 0. So it was join, and its blast radius was the module."
    :after "with join moved here, that same probe is exit 0 on wasm32 AND web at amu 9092ee34 -- the amu that could not build it before -- and both artifacts RUN, answering 1. The join probe itself is exit 70 at 9092ee34 (\"unsupported typed Wasm expression\" on wasm32, \"unsupported KIR node\" on web) and exit 0 on both at an amu carrying kotoba-script f803255e / kotoba-wasm 829966fd, where it RUNS: (join \",\" (typed-list-new [:list :string] \"a\" \"bb\" \"ccc\")) = \"a,bb,ccc\", with starts-with? still 1 in the same artifact."
    :removal-condition "when the consuming trees run an amu whose kotoba-script pin is at or past f803255ed259203e008d989f859b3b1b5fd22c98 and whose kotoba-wasm pin is at or past 829966fd47148d3ef2b873ea35bed57a63928606 (amu PR #898, merged to amu main as 8cc1361fa90ae084b4b5a3ef019caee9a1a097bb on 2026-09-08), this folds into kotoba.string and the namespace is retired. The function does not change; only which module can afford to hold it."
    :arity "the TWO-argument arity only: (join separator items). clojure.string/join also has (join coll), which concatenates with no separator; there is no multi-arity here, so that call is an arity refusal at check time. Asserted in the oracle test rather than described."
    :oracle "clojure.string/join itself, on the same separator and the same items: 16 cases covering the empty, single and many shapes, an empty separator, empty items, multi-byte separators and items, and an astral one. The test prints JOIN-CHECKED as failures*1000 + cases, so a run that checked nothing (0) is distinguishable from a clean run (16) and from one regression (1016)."}
   :costs {:join "one string-concat per item after the first, onto an accumulator rebuilt each step -- O(result length x item count) bytes copied. The list is bounded by construction (kotoba-sema max-list-items is 128) and the recursion is fuel-bounded like any other guest recursion."}
   :hazards
   {:join-takes-a-list-not-a-seq "join's second argument is a [:list :string], not any sequence. Clojure's join takes any coll and calls str on each element; this takes strings only, and the element type is in the descriptor. What a caller can actually PASS is narrow today: a literal typed-list-new, or a typed-map-keys / typed-map-vals projection (:measured :list-construction)."}}
  :clojure.core
  {:path "lang/compat/clojure/core.kotoba"
   :provides #{ex-info ex-message ex-data}
   :equivalence :document-value-not-a-throwable
   :why "The abort ability carries a `:document` as its error type E, so the
         shape a .cljc module already writes -- a message and a data map,
         thrown, caught, read back -- moves across without changing at the call
         sites. What does NOT move across is the Throwable: clojure.core/ex-info
         returns an ExceptionInfo with a class, a stack trace and a cause, and
         this returns an ordinary two-entry document. It is not an approximation
         of a Throwable; it answers the three questions the trio is used for."
   :measured
   {:as-of "2026-09-02"
    :document-error-type "(throw (ex-info \"empty input\" (document-map ...))) gives the throwing function the interface [:result :i64 :document] and the row #{:abort}; a bare `catch` binds the document and an explicit `(catch :document e ...)` pins it. NO frontend work was needed for this -- the E was already whatever the throw's operand types as. Measured against kotoba-sema f7c56ab3."
    :propagation "the document E survives slice-2 propagation: `read2` writes no throw and no try, calls `parse`, and the catcher two calls away reads the same message back"
    :oracle "clojure.core/ex-info itself, called from test/kotoba/lang/clojure_core_compat_test.cljk on the same message and data, asked the same two questions; the divergences below are asserted against it rather than described"
    :data-literal "(ex-info \"m\" {:input s}) does NOT compile -- a map literal is a constant document and `s` is a string local, refused with \"expression type mismatch: expected i64, got string\". The data argument is built with `document-map`."}
   :hazards
   {:no-nil "clojure.core answers nil for a missing message and for the data of a non-ExceptionInfo. There is no nil here: a document with no :message reads as \"\" and one with no :data as (document-null), which counts as empty. A caller testing for nil has to change the test."
    :non-map-traps "(ex-message e) and (ex-data e) TRAP with document-map-required on a document that is not a map, where clojure.core/ex-message ANSWERS for any Throwable. Nothing is silently wrong -- the program stops -- but it stops where Clojure would have returned a value."
    :arity "the one-argument (message only) and three-argument (message, data, cause) arities of ex-info are absent: a cause is another Throwable, and there is no Throwable here to be one. BOTH directions are now refused, and the message says the counts: (ex-info \"m\") gives \"function call arity mismatch: ex-info takes 2 arguments; got 1\" and (ex-info \"m\" data cause) gives \"... got 3\". Until 2026-09-02 the two were NOT symmetric -- the over-arity call was admitted and the third argument dropped without a word, which was a general hole in kotoba-sema rather than anything about this module ((two 1 2 3) on (defn- two [a b] (+ a b)) answered 3). kotoba-sema ADR 0011 closed it; the entry that pinned the hole, in test/kotoba/lang/clojure_core_compat_test.cljk, now pins its absence."
    :not-thrown-by-clojure "the document this builds is thrown by Kotoba's typed `throw`, which lowers to a result value. It never becomes a JVM exception, so nothing outside the guest can catch it as one."}
   :absent
   {ex-cause {:reason "a cause is a Throwable and there is none here."}
    Throwable->map {:reason "serialises a Throwable's class, message, data, cause chain and stack trace. Four of those five do not exist in this language."}}}

  ;; The four finite-set operations the compiler's own sources call, over
  ;; the language's typed set. A TEMPLATE: written once over `elem`,
  ;; instantiated per element type by amu's project linker.
  :clojure.set
  {:path "lang/compat/clojure/set.kotoba"
   :kind :template
   :provides #{union difference intersection subset?}
   :equivalence :exact
   :arities {union 2 difference 2 intersection 2 subset? 2}
   :params [elem]
   :default-binding {elem :keyword}
   :why-a-default
   "The 16 compiler modules that require clojure.set are DUAL-RUNTIME source: they keep loading on nbb / Clojure while being admitted here, and their `(:require [clojure.set :as set])` cannot carry `:with`. Measured 2026-09-11: ClojureScript 1.12.42 refuses `[clojure.set :as set :with {elem :keyword}]` -- \"Only :as, :refer and :rename options supported in :require / :require-macros; offending spec: ...\"; Clojure 1.12.5 (`clojure -M -e`) and nbb 1.4.208 (`nbb -e`) load it and print the union, because both ignore libspec options they do not know -- not a place to hang a meaning. So the template declares the binding a bare require means. Nothing is inferred from call sites: the require without :with IS the require with :with {elem :keyword} (same instantiation key, same linked text, same definition CIDs, measured by amu's test/nbb/project.cljk `the-default-binding-and-the-spelled-out-one-are-one-instantiation`)."
   :why-keyword
   "Surveyed 2026-09-11 over the 16 modules on the compiler's own classpath that require clojure.set (115 call sites: difference 38, union 21, intersection 15, subset? 11, the rest 1-2 each): the operands are effect rows, capability ids and map-key sets -- keyword sets -- in all but three modules (symbol heads in kotoba-sema's frontend, CID strings in amu's package lock, path strings in amu's coverage). Those three are refused at the first non-keyword call, by type."
   :measured
   {:as-of "2026-09-11"
    :amu "the default-binding linker, kotoba-lang/amu PR after 29502e5b"
    :probe "a 2-module project `(ns app {:kotoba/export [main]} (:require [clojure.set :as set])) (defn main [] :i64 (+ (* 1000 (count (set/union #{:a :b} #{:b :c}))) (* 100 (count (set/difference #{:a :b :c} #{:b}))) (* 10 (count (set/intersection #{:a :b :c} #{:b :c :d}))) (if (set/subset? #{:a} #{:a :b}) 1 0)))` through `amu check --source-path <app> --source-path lang/compat --jvm-free` is :ok true with :module-order [[clojure.set {elem :keyword}] app], and `main` answers 3221 on the KIR interpreter"
    :before "the same project without lang/compat on the source path: `required module is missing from the explicit source paths` (exit 65) -- the wall this closes, 34 of the compiler's 208 sources at amu 6e56b1d8"
    :empty-module-control "an EMPTY clojure.set module (the shape a frontend-builtin design would need) cannot exist: `(ns clojure.set (:export []))` is refused `at least one defn is required`, and with a private placeholder defn `entryless library requires at least one exported function`"
    :oracle "clojure.set itself, from test/kotoba/lang/clojure_set_compat_test.cljk: every pair of subsets of [:a :b :c :d :e] (1,024 pairs) for each of the four functions, sets crossing the interpreter as bit masks; the counts the test prints are the record"
    :wrong-element-type "`(set/union (typed-set-new [:set :string] \"a\") (typed-set-new [:set :string] \"b\"))` under the default is refused at the call site: `expression type mismatch: expected [:set :keyword], got [:set :string]` -- by type, never by a different answer"
    :older-amu "an amu without the default-binding linker refuses this file by name: `namespace :params must be a non-empty vector of distinct simple symbols` (measured at 29502e5b). A consumer on an older amu gets a refusal, not a misread."}
   :hazards
   {:one-binding-per-alias "a module that unions keyword sets in one function and symbol sets in another needs two aliases, `[clojure.set :as set] [clojure.set :as symset :with {elem :symbol}]` -- and the second is Kotoba-only source. Under the bare require the symbol call is refused by type at its call site; the message names the expected [:set :keyword] but not the template that fixed it."
    :function-as-value "`(reduce set/union #{} xs)` and `(apply set/union ...)` pass the import as a VALUE; the linker rewrites qualified CALL heads only, so these reach the frontend as an unbound symbol. A separate wall, not this file's."
    :cost "union / difference walk s2 by `typed-set-nth` (index), intersection / subset? walk s1; each step is one fuel-bounded loop iteration and one set primitive. Bounded sets, so this is fine; it is not the hashed O(1) membership the host has."
    :no-nil "clojure.set/union with a nil operand answers the other set; there is no nil here, and an empty typed set is `(typed-set-new [:set elem])`."}
   :absent
   {superset? {:reason "subset? with its arguments swapped. Absent until a consumer calls it -- the one call site on the compiler's classpath is kotoba.lang.coll, which MIRRORS clojure.set rather than consuming it."}
    select {:reason "takes a predicate as a value and a set to filter; a function value crossing a module boundary has no typed spelling in this subset yet."}
    project {:reason "a relation -- a set of maps -- projected onto keys. A set of maps is not a typed set this language spells ([:set [:map ...]] is not an admitted element type), so the operand cannot be written."}
    rename-keys {:reason "a map with keys renamed through a map; not a set operation, and the keyword-keyed map literal is a closed record here, whose keys are fixed by its schema."}
    rename {:reason "rename-keys over every map of a relation; absent for both of those reasons."}
    index {:reason "a relation indexed into a map from key-projection to sub-relation; the operand and the result are both shapes this subset does not spell."}
    map-invert {:reason "a map with keys and values swapped; the canonical typed map [:map K V] could carry it, but it is not a set operation and no consumer on the compiler's classpath calls it outside kotoba.lang.coll's mirror."}
    join {:reason "a natural join of two relations; relations are absent, see project."}}}

  ;; Not a Clojure namespace. The honest counterpart to one of the absent
  ;; names above, under a name that says which kind of index it answers.
  :kotoba.string
  {:path "lang/compat/kotoba/string.kotoba"
   :kind :canonical
   :provides #{byte-index-of utf16-index-of utf16-last-index-of
               starts-with? ends-with? includes?
               blank? trim triml trimr reverse
               index-of last-index-of
               replace}
   :moved-here
   {:as-of "2026-09-08"
    :names #{starts-with? ends-with? includes? blank? trim triml trimr reverse}
    :from clojure.string
    :why "owner decision 2026-09-08: kotoba.string is the canonical guest string library. clojure.string becomes a temporary shim over it (see that entry) and kotoba.text an alias over it."
    :what-changed "nothing about the functions. They are byte-for-byte the definitions that were in lang/compat/clojure/string.kotoba, held by the same oracle -- clojure.string itself, on the same inputs. What changed is which namespace owns them, and that one duplicate private helper (code-point-width, which both modules had identically) became one."
    :join-did-not-come "join is in kotoba.string.join. It reads a [:list :string] with typed-list-nth, and a module carrying that operation is refused WHOLE by an amu that does not lower it -- so putting it here would move the blast radius rather than remove it. Measured; see :modules :kotoba.string.join :measured."}
   :replaces {byte-index-of clojure.string/index-of
              utf16-index-of clojure.string/index-of
              utf16-last-index-of clojure.string/last-index-of}
   :equivalence {byte-index-of :byte-offset-not-character-index
                 utf16-index-of :clojure-index-with-minus-one-for-nil
                 utf16-last-index-of :clojure-index-with-minus-one-for-nil}
   :measured
   {:as-of "2026-09-01"
    :oracle "a naive search over (.getBytes s \"UTF-8\") written independently of the Kotoba source, in test/kotoba/lang/kotoba_string_compat_test.cljk; 28 inputs, all agreeing, 18 of them with a multi-byte haystack"
    :divergence "(byte-index-of \"aあb\" \"b\") = 4 where (clojure.string/index-of \"aあb\" \"b\") = 2; asserted, not assumed, so the argument for the name cannot quietly evaporate"
    :route "amu check <entry> --source-path <app> --source-path lang/compat --jvm-free, exit 0, module-order [kotoba.string app]"
    :executed "not only lowered: compiled to wasm32 (2,279 bytes, exit 0, JVM-free), instantiated through amu runtime/browser-host.mjs, main() = 4n for (byte-index-of \"a\u3042b\" \"b\") and 3n for (byte-index-of \"\u3042a\" \"a\"). wasm32-browser also compiles (2,292 bytes)."
    :utf16 {:as-of "2026-09-02"
            :amu "bb51dc14"
            :oracle "clojure.string/index-of and last-index-of themselves, nil read as -1, on the byte-index-of input set plus the astral cases; asserted equal, and asserted DIFFERENT from byte-index-of where a multi-byte code point precedes the match"
            :executed "a three-module project (clojure.string, kotoba.string, app) compiled to wasm32 (4,426 bytes, exit 0, JVM-free) and instantiated through amu runtime/browser-host.mjs: (utf16-index-of \"a\ud83d\ude00b\" \"b\") = 3, (utf16-last-index-of \"abab\" \"ab\") = 2, (utf16-last-index-of \"a\ud83d\ude00\" \"\") = 3"}}
   :costs
   {:ends-with? "walks the haystack once by code point to find whether the suffix begins on a boundary; recursion here is fuel-bounded like any other guest recursion"
    :trim "two walks (left edge, then right edge from there); triml and trimr one each; blank? one, stopping at the first non-whitespace code point"
    :reverse "one walk with one string-concat and one string-substring per code point"
    :utf16-index-of "the byte-index-of scan, carrying a UTF-16 unit count alongside the byte offset; utf16-last-index-of tries every boundary to the end and keeps the last match, so it never short-circuits"
    :byte-index-of "a naive scan: every code-point boundary of the haystack in turn, and at each one a code-point-by-code-point compare until the needle ends or a pair differs -- O(haystack code points x needle code points) steps worst case, and one string-code-point-at per compared code point. Recursion here is fuel-bounded like any other guest recursion."}
   :hazards
   {:substring-prefix "(string-substring \"あ\" 0 1) refuses, so a substring-based starts-with? TRAPS where the answer is false. Compared by code point instead."
    :empty-needle-and-includes? "string-contains? refuses an empty needle (empty-string-search-needle) where clojure.string answers true, which is why includes? is not an alias. Named apart from :empty-needle below, which is byte-index-of's answer to the same question: the two hazards collided under one key when these functions moved here on 2026-09-08, and the EDN reader caught it -- Duplicate key: :empty-needle."
    :arity "each provided function is the one-string arity only. clojure.string's nil-accepting behaviour (blank? nil is true) has no counterpart because there is no nil string; a caller passing a second argument gets an arity refusal at check time, never a different answer."
    :cljc-plane-same-name "there is also a kotoba.string in the .cljc plane, with a LARGER surface. This guest surface is deliberately a SUBSET of it, and the shared names answer the same thing on the same inputs -- held by the oracle, which calls clojure.string itself rather than a table. Where a guest function deliberately diverges from its cljc counterpart, the divergence is named here."
    :no-contains-precheck "string-contains? would short-circuit an absent needle, but it answers the same question by a second mechanism; if the two ever disagreed this function would return -1 while a match existed. One slower mechanism beats two that must agree."
    :empty-needle "answers 0, matching clojure.string/index-of, and handled before the scan because string-contains? refuses an empty needle."
    :self-synchronising "the scan only looks at code-point boundaries, and that loses nothing: a valid UTF-8 needle begins with an ASCII byte or a lead byte, never a continuation byte, so a byte-level occurrence cannot start inside a code point."
    :minus-one-is-not-nil "utf16-index-of answers -1 where clojure.string/index-of answers nil. A caller who threads the result into arithmetic gets -1 where Clojure would have thrown; the name is the warning, and the option-valued spelling is waiting on :measured :option-return-on-wasm32."
    :not-a-byte-offset "the UTF-16 index is not usable with string-substring or string-code-point-at; (string-substring s 0 (utf16-index-of s x)) is the byte-offset defect in the other direction. byte-index-of is the one that composes."}
   :added {:as-of "2026-09-02"
           :names #{blank? trim triml trimr reverse}
           :whitespace "java.lang.Character.isWhitespace (JDK 21, Unicode 15) written out as a bounded predicate over code points: U+0009..U+000D, U+001C..U+001F, U+0020, U+1680, U+2000..U+2006, U+2008..U+200A, U+2028, U+2029, U+205F, U+3000. Deliberately NOT in it: U+00A0, U+2007, U+202F (non-breaking, which Java excludes) and U+0085 (NEL, which Java never counted). The oracle sweep is what holds that table to the JVM; if a JDK moves the set the sweep goes red and the table follows."
           :reverse "java.lang.StringBuilder.reverse keeps surrogate pairs together, so clojure.string/reverse is a code-point reversal and this one agrees on astral input; asserted with U+1F600 in the oracle, not assumed."}
