   ;; reason recorded from 2026-09-01 to that day was never about strings: the
   ;; index half was answered (utf16-index-of, measured 2026-09-02 against
   ;; clojure.string itself), and what kept the Clojure names out was the nil
   ;; half, whose exact spelling is an option-valued return -- which refused to
   ;; lower to wasm32 for the WHOLE project, reached or not
   ;; (:measured :option-return-on-wasm32). Re-measured at amu 9092ee34 that is
   ;; false, so the names are provided, option-valued. They live HERE rather
   ;; than in clojure.string because of the same-day move that made this the
   ;; canonical module; clojure.string forwards them like everything else.
   :landed
   {index-of
    {:as-of "2026-09-08"
     :returns "[:option :i64] -- the UTF-16 code-unit index clojure.string returns, and Clojure's nil as the absence it is"
     :body "kotoba.string/utf16-index-of, whose -1 sentinel is wrapped back into the option. The scan is NOT repeated: two mechanisms that must agree is the defect this file refuses elsewhere, so there is one scan and it lives in kotoba.string, reached through (:require [kotoba.string :as ks])."
     :arity "the two-argument arity only, as everywhere else in this module. clojure.string/index-of also takes a from-index, and also takes a CHARACTER rather than a string; neither has a counterpart here, and both are an arity or type refusal at check time rather than a different answer."
     :oracle "clojure.string/index-of itself, called, with nil as the absent case -- not a table of expected numbers. test/kotoba/lang/clojure_string_option_index_test.cljk, 20 input pairs across both functions, present and absent, ASCII and multi-byte and astral."
     :divergence "none on the two-string arity. The absent case is the option's none where Clojure's is nil, which is the same answer in the type system this guest has: a caller cannot thread it into arithmetic, where Clojure would have thrown. That is the whole gain over kotoba.string/utf16-index-of's -1, which a caller CAN thread into +."
     :measured "2026-09-08: agrees with clojure.string/index-of on every oracle input, nil included. Compiled through amu 9092ee34 to wasm32, wasm32-browser and web from a real project (--source-path lang/compat, so the :require resolves for real) and RUN: a 12-check self-check returning failures*1000 + checks answered 12 on all three targets -- 0 failures, 12 checks. Deliberately regressed twice to show the checks discriminate: dropping the -1 test from found-or-absent gave 4012 (the 4 absent cases, and a boolean could not have told 1 from 4), and the JVM oracle went to 15 failures reading (not (= nil -1))."
     :cost "exactly kotoba.string/utf16-index-of's -- one naive scan, O(haystack code points x needle code points) worst case, fuel-bounded like any guest recursion. The wrapper adds one comparison."}
    last-index-of
    {:as-of "2026-09-08"
     :returns "[:option :i64], same as index-of"
     :body "kotoba.string/utf16-last-index-of, same -1 wrap"
     :oracle "clojure.string/last-index-of itself, nil included, on the same 20 input pairs"
     :divergence "none on the two-string arity. The empty needle answers the UTF-16 LENGTH of the haystack, which is what String.lastIndexOf of the empty string answers -- 3 for a + U+1F600, not 2 -- asserted against the JVM rather than assumed, because that is one of the two cases a believed table gets written wrong. The other is that an astral code point is TWO units, so index-of of b in a + U+1F600 + b is 3."
     :measured "2026-09-08, in the same oracle run and the same three-target compiled artifact as index-of."}}
   :absent
   {lower-case
    {:reason "TWO independent reasons, and the table is no longer one of them. (1) LOCALE: clojure.string/lower-case is (.. s toString toLowerCase) -- no locale argument, so Locale.getDefault(). (.toLowerCase \"I\" (Locale/forLanguageTag \"tr\")) is \"\u0131\" where Locale/ROOT gives \"i\", asserted in the oracle. A Kotoba guest has no locale to read, so under this name the answer would depend on something the guest cannot see -- ambient authority, in the plainest form. (2) FINAL SIGMA: String.toLowerCase(ROOT) lowercases a word-final \u03a3 to \u03c2 -- \"\u0391\u03a3\" becomes \"\u03b1\u03c2\" and \"\u0391\u03a3\u0392\" becomes \"\u03b1\u03c3\u03b2\" -- which is a property of the SURROUNDING string, not of the code point, and deciding it needs the Cased and Case_Ignorable properties over the whole of Unicode. Plus U+0130, which String lowercases to TWO characters where Character.toLowerCase gives one."
     :what-would-be-needed "for lower-case-root: Cased and Case_Ignorable tables and the Final_Sigma condition. For the Clojure name itself: a locale, which a guest does not have."
     :instead kotoba.string.case/simple-lower-case
     :measured "2026-09-02: simple-lower-case agrees with the Unicode SIMPLE mapping code point by code point over the sweep (every one below U+3100 plus every one the JVM maps anywhere plus the Unicode 16.0 rows a Unicode-15 JDK lacks). Character.toLowerCase(int) is the oracle where the host JDK is current; on Temurin 21 it answers identity for U+019B / U+0264 / U+1C89 / U+1C8A and Kotoba follows Unicode, asserted rather than treated as a JVM defect. The two divergences from String.toLowerCase(Locale.ROOT) -- final sigma and U+0130 -- are asserted rather than described, in test/kotoba/lang/kotoba_string_case_test.cljk."}
    upper-case
    {:reason "LOCALE, and only locale. Unlike lower-case there is no conditional rule left: kotoba.string.case/upper-case-root is exact against String.toUpperCase(Locale.ROOT), expansions included. What keeps the Clojure name out is that it calls .toUpperCase() with no locale, so (.toUpperCase \"i\" (Locale/forLanguageTag \"tr\")) is \"\u0130\" where ROOT gives \"I\"."
     :what-would-be-needed "a locale, which a guest does not have. Nothing else."
     :instead #{kotoba.string.case/upper-case-root kotoba.string.case/simple-upper-case}
     :measured "2026-09-02: upper-case-root agrees with String.toUpperCase(Locale.ROOT) over the sweep AND over 244 multi-code-point strings (44 hand-picked, 200 from seed 20260902), except where the host JDK's simple mapping is behind Unicode 16.0 -- then the published simple uppercase, asserted. The string sweep is separate because a claim about a string is not a claim about a character. The 102 SpecialCasing expansions (\u00df -> SS, \u0149 -> \u02bcN, \ufb01 -> FI) are in the table; simple-upper-case is the one-code-point mapping and leaves \u00df alone, which the oracle asserts."}
    capitalize
    {:reason "THREE reasons. The two from lower-case (locale, and the String-level mappings), plus its own: clojure.string/capitalize is (str (.toUpperCase (subs s 0 1)) (.toLowerCase (subs s 1))), and (subs s 0 1) is one UTF-16 CODE UNIT. When the first character is astral that is half a surrogate pair, so (clojure.string/capitalize \"\ud801\udc28bc\") is \"\ud801\udc28bc\" -- unchanged, because .toUpperCase of a lone high surrogate is itself and the halves concatenate back. Measured, not read off the source, because the defect leaves no trace on most inputs."
     :instead kotoba.string.case/simple-capitalize
     :measured "2026-09-02: simple-capitalize uppercases the first CODE POINT (\"\ud801\udc28bc\" -> \"\ud801\udc00bc\", where clojure.string/capitalize answers \"\ud801\udc28bc\"), and the divergence is asserted."}
    split
    {:reason "TWO reasons as of 2026-09-08, and neither is the one recorded on 2026-09-02. The accessor exists and lowers on both backends now (:measured :list-of-string), so a list CAN be read back. (1) CONSTRUCTION: a split returns as many segments as the input has, and a [:list T] can only be constructed with a statically known item count -- there is no conj, cons or append on one (:measured :list-construction). (2) THE PATTERN: clojure.string/split does not take a string separator at all. Measured 2026-09-08 on Clojure 1.12.4, (clojure.string/split \"a,b\" \",\") throws ClassCastException: class java.lang.String cannot be cast to class java.util.regex.Pattern. So a string-separator split is a DIFFERENT function from clojure.string/split, and by this module's own rule it does not get the Clojure name -- it would belong in kotoba.string under a name that says it splits on a literal, the same shape as byte-index-of."
     :what-would-be-needed "for the Clojure name: a regex, which is a reader rejection here (:measured :regex-literal). For a literal-separator split under a kotoba.string name: a way to build a list of unknown length. The nearest existing one is a [:map :i64 :string] built by typed-map-assoc and projected with typed-map-vals, which lowers on the web backend and NOT on wasm32 (:measured :list-construction) -- so it would move this module to a lowering that does not exist yet, which is the defect the :absent index-of entry refuses. The scan itself is written and measured in a scratch file (a,b,,c on , counts 4 segments); it is the return VALUE, still, that has no spelling."
     :measured-oracle "clojure.string/split's own behaviour, recorded so the eventual implementation has a target rather than a belief: (split \"a,b,,c,\" #\",\") is [\"a\" \"b\" \"\" \"c\"] -- trailing empties dropped; (split \"\" #\",\") is [\"\"]; (split \",a\" #\",\") is [\"\" \"a\"]; (split \",,,\" #\",\") is []; (split \"abc\" #\",\") is [\"abc\"]. Measured 2026-09-08, Clojure 1.12.4."}
    split-lines {:reason "same return as split: as many segments as the input has, and no way to build a list of unknown length (:measured :list-construction). Its separator question is smaller than split's -- it splits on a newline, optionally preceded by a carriage return, rather than on an arbitrary pattern -- so construction is the whole of what is left."}
    replace-first {:reason "the literal arity's first-occurrence half. `replace` (all occurrences) is provided as of 2026-09-12; this one is not, because string-replace-all is all occurrences and no consumer on the compiler's classpath calls replace-first with a string match -- the one call site (amu's test_profile.cljk) passes a regex, which is a reader rejection. kotoba.lang.bounded-text/replace-first-text in kotoba-lang/text is the scan, should a caller appear."}}
   ;; replace moved OUT of :absent on 2026-09-12. Its reason from 2026-09-02
   ;; ended "nothing in this workspace has asked for it yet"; the self-host
